That's my photo, reduced to 64×64 pixels and compiled to Based64, a Base64 + ASCII combination. The text not only compresses the images, it provides a preview of the image, and editing it edits the images.

# Try Now

Choose an image, GIF, video, or audio file. Edit the text and watch it change. Everything happens locally.

based64Studio

# Less media, same idea

Based64 keeps at most 64×64 pixels and 64 colors per frame. Enough for a face, a diagram, or a short instructional animation.

# Simple example:

Below is an image of an arrow:

based64
 ...$...
 ..$$$..
 .$$$$$.
 ...$...

Use `based64 animation` for a flipbook or `based64 audio` for sound. Dimensions are inferred. Optional `colors`, `delay`, and `rate` settings live inside the block; animation frames start with `frame`.

Spaces, unfamiliar characters, ragged rows—even an empty drawing—still render. Short rows get padded; oversized drawings get cropped. Scramble away.

One character per pixel. A shared palette keeps animations small. We deliberately throw away detail instead of preserving the original file.

The parser and tests are single Scroll files.
 ../microlangs/based64.scroll parser
 ../tests/based64.scroll tests

Now ships in Scroll 179.0.0.

code
 npm install -g http://github.com/breck7/scroll

